- The 26 parameter names/bounds/ranges must come from you or the paper before the design is executable.

**Design choices I propose (flag any you want changed):**
- **Parametric-only inputs.** Because we ask about 26-parameter surrogates, meshes/point clouds/pressure fields are out of scope; therefore no CNN/GNO/UNet/point-net architectures. Reconciliation: architecture differences are compared *within the tabular family* (linear/kernel/tree/MLP/transformer-style), which is also the honest comparison for the question as posed. If you want the field-based nets included, that changes the question and the compute story.
- **Target = continuous Cd regression**, not a discretized class-of-excellence label; "predictive performance" = held-out regression error, with the within-5/10-count rates as a derived, reporting-level proxy for CoE-style decisions. (CdA can be reported as a linear rescaling; front/rear CdA decomposition is not assumed available.)
- **Partition integrity**: one frozen pool; nested subsets are strict supersets via a single permutation per seed; the same frozen VAL and TEST for every N and every architecture; TEST never used for early stopping, scaler fitting, or tuning; final evaluation run once.
- **Preprocessing**: input standardization fit **once on the full TRAIN pool and frozen**, so the input distribution is identical at every N (avoids scaler-as-confound); a sensitivity arm refits the scaler per-subset to confirm the choice doesn't drive the result. No target transform for the primary run (Cd range is narrow and near-homoscedastic); log-Cd as a one-off sensitivity check.
- **Budget fairness**: equalized per-(arch, N) hyperparameter search budget (e.g. ~10 random-search trials on VAL, or one shared config per arch) — otherwise "architecture effect" is partly "tuning-effort effect". Early stopping only where meaningful (MLP), on the frozen VAL.
