", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": null, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-ae653e2f8d6bd5e0", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-ae653e2f8d6bd5e0", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{"answer": "# FINAL EXPERIMENT DESIGN (design-only; nothing has been run — no results are reported or implied)\n\n**Question.** Does surrogate predictive performance for aerodynamic drag improve with training-set size, and does that scaling behaviour differ across model architectures? Two-factor learning-curve design: **architecture × training-set size**, parametric-only inputs, one frozen TEST set.\n\n**Global conventions.** All dataset-dependent numbers are written as `[A-n]` assumptions with the confirmation each still needs. All wall-clock numbers are order-of-magnitude design estimates, not measurements. Every number marked **[V]** was verified by inspecting this machine.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Data model, split, and nesting protocol\n\n**Unit of analysis.** One sample = 26 geometric parameters `x ∈ R²⁶` → scalar total drag coefficient `Cd` from the CFD solve. Parametric-only: no mesh, point cloud, pressure or wall-shear fields.\n\n**Pool and split.** Assume the DrivAerNet++ parametric table (parameters + force-derived coefficients) is the only ingested artifact. No canonical public *parametric* split is relied upon; we build and freeze our own:\n- `TRAIN` / `VAL` / `TEST` = 80 / 10 / 10 % of the pool, stratified on body variant (notchback / estateback / fastback) → `[A-1]` pool ≈ 10,000 samples → ≈ 8,000 / 1,000 / 1,000. *Needs confirmation: actual sample count and the column that encodes body variant.*\n- Split generated once from seed 20261023, written to `split_v1.csv` (sample id → partition, stratum), hash-logged, and reused by every run. TEST is never regenerated.\n- `[A-2]` Target = total `Cd` (dimensionless). Front/rear CdA decomposition is not assumed available. *Needs confirmation: exact target column and whether CdA is derived by a fixed reference area — if so, CdA is reported as a linear rescaling of the same prediction.*\n- `[A-3]` Driving conditions (yaw angle, road level) are separate from the 26 geometric parameters in DrivAerNet++. Primary arm: restrict the pool to the nominal (0° yaw) subset so that `x → Cd` is a well-defined function; the 26 geometric parameters are the only features. *Needs confirmation: (i) the 26 parameter names/bounds, (ii) how many samples exist at nominal yaw. If yaw-swept samples dominate, the primary arm becomes \"all yaw angles, yaw included as an extra input\" and this is stated as a design change, not silently absorbed.*\n\n**Nested subsets (the core integrity device).** For each seed `s ∈ {0,1,2,3,4}`: partition `TRAIN` by body variant; permute each stratum with `RandomState(1000+s)`; subset `N_k` = first `round(N_k · stratum_share)` members of each stratum. Consequence: `S_1 ⊂ S_2 ⊂ … ⊂ S_K` as **strict supersets** with body-variant composition constant to <1 sample across all `N`. Asserted at runtime (`assert S_k ⊆ S_{k+1}`, stratum share within tolerance, `id(S_K) ∩ id(TEST) = ∅`).\n\n**Size ladder** (log-spaced, 8 points): `N ∈ {50, 100, 200, 400, 800, 1600, 3200, N_max}` with `N_max = |TRAIN|` ≈ 6,400–8,000 `[A-1]`. Ladder chosen so consecutive points are ~2× apart, which is what makes a power-law exponent estimable.\n\n**Preprocessing.** Inputs standardized with mean/std fit **once on the full TRAIN pool**, frozen for every N, every architecture, and reused unchanged on VAL/TEST. Target: raw `Cd`, no transform. Sensitivity arms (not primary): log-Cd target; per-subset scaler refit.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Architectures (the \"architecture\" factor, explicitly scoped)\n\nBecause inputs are 26-dim vectors, **\"differ across model architectures\" is scoped to the tabular family**; CNN/GNO/point-net/field-based surrogates are out of scope by construction (they answer a different question and would need data and compute this task does not have). Arms:\n\n| ID | Architecture | Role |\n|---|---|---|\n| A0 | Constant (train-mean Cd) | no-signal **floor**, no fit |\n| A1 | Ridge / elastic-net linear | linearity floor, monotone reference |\n| A2 | Kernel ridge, RBF (with exact GP, ARD-Matern-5/2, capped at N ≤ 1,600; Nyström with m = 500 above) | strong small-N + predictive uncertainty |\n| A3 | Random forest (600 trees, min-samples-leaf tuned) | bagging family |\n| A4 | Gradient-boosted trees (HistGB/LightGBM/XGBoost) | cheapest tabular front-runner; also used in the secondary arm |\n| A5 | MLP-mid, 2×128, standard dense, early stopping | standard deep tabular |\n| A6 | MLP-deep, 4×256 residual + LayerNorm | capacity/depth probe |\n| A7 | FT-Transformer (d=64, 3 layers, 4 heads) | one high-capacity tabular net |\n| A-null | Shuffled-label control on A4 and A6 | pipeline-artifact control |\n\nPer-arm search spaces are fixed in a config manifest before any run: A1 α ∈ log-U(1e-3,1e3); A2 γ ∈ log-U(2⁻⁴,2²), α ∈ log-U(1e-3,1e2); A3 max-features ∈ {0.3,0.5,0.7,1.0}, min-samples-leaf ∈ {1,2,4,8}; A4 n-estimates ≤ 2,000 w/ early stopping, lr ∈ log-U(0.01,0.3), depth ∈ {3,5,7}; A5/A6 width/depth/lr/dropout/weight-decay; A7 d/head/lr/dropout. Early stopping (A5–A7) uses **only** the frozen VAL.\n\n**Fairness rule.** Exactly **10 validation-driven trials per (architecture, N, seed)** for every tunable arm — a fixed budget independent of N and of architecture — then one refit on `S_N` with the VAL-best config and the same seed. A1 and A0 are exempt (≤2 and 0 trials) and this exemption is documented as such, not discovered post hoc.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Metrics\n\n- **Primary:** test **RMSE** on Cd.\n- **Co-primary reporting:** MAE, R², and **Cd-count error** (RMSE/MAE expressed in counts, 1 count = 0.001 Cd).\n- **Derived decision views:** fraction of test predictions within ≤5 and ≤10 counts; NRMSE/MAPE (secondary, since Cd range is narrow `[A-4]` ≈ 0.23–0.50, MAPE is near-proportional to MAE).\n- **Uncertainty calibration (A2 only, descriptive):** NLL / PICP@90.\n- No class-of-excellence label is used as a first-class endpoint.\n- **Noise-floor probe:** duplicate / near-duplicate parameter vectors in the pool (if any) give a direct CFD-repeatability estimate; otherwise the A2 predictive variance and A4/A6 N_max residuals bound the plausible floor. Reported alongside every curve so \"saturation\" can be read against a floor rather than against zero.\n- All metrics computed from saved per-cell prediction arrays (`preds/{arch}_{N}_{seed}_{regime}.npz`), so metric tables can be recomputed without refitting.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Runs, cost, and feasibility\n\nCost table (arithmetic checked with a stdlib script; per-fit seconds are estimates):\n\n| Arm | Fits (top level) | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Primary 7 trainable archs × 8 sizes × 5 seeds | **280** | + ~2,400 inner tuning fits (10 trials × 240 tunable cells) |\n| A0 floor evals 8 × 5 | 40 | no fit |\n| A-null shuffled labels 2 × 4 × 3 | 24 | sizes {100,400,1600,N_max} |\n| log-Cd sensitivity 2 × 4 × 3 | 24 | |\n| Per-subset scaler-refit sensitivity 2 × 4 × 3 | 24 | |\n| **Secondary regime arm** 2 archs (A4, A5) × 4 sizes × 3 seeds × 2 regimes | 48 | new pool for the extrapolation regime → all cells new |\n| **Total** | **≈ 440 top-level fits + ≈ 2,400 tuning fits** | |\n\nWall-clock reasoning (estimates): ridge/RF/HGB seconds per fit; KRR at N_max is an O(N³) solve (6,400³ ≈ 2.6e11 flops) → tens of seconds, acceptable; exact GP capped at N ≤ 1,600 and Nyström above (this is the reason for the cap). MLPs on 8k×26 with CPU PyTorch: ~30–60 s per trial fit → A5+A6 with 10 trials ≈ 10 h sequential; A7 ≈ 13 h sequential. Deep arms therefore dominate at ~23 h single-threaded-equivalent and land at **~1–2 h wall clock** using ~48–56 workers × 4 threads on 224 cores; whole suite ≈ **half a day wall clock, a few dozen core-hours**, ~2 TB RAM is never binding (largest dense object ~330 MB).\n\n**Environment as verified [V]:** `/workspace` contains only `task.json`; no DrivAerNet++ data, metadata, split files, scripts or model code anywhere on the filesystem; Python 3.14.4 with **no** numpy/scipy/pandas/sklearn/torch/xgboost/lightgbm/h5py; pip unusable offline and DNS resolution fails (network disabled); no GPU; 224 CPUs; ~1,931 GB free RAM; `/workspace` writable.\n\n**Requirement on any future run (a prerequisite, not a finding):** `numpy`, `scikit-learn`, `scipy`, `h5py`/`pandas`, CPU `torch`, and one GBT library, plus the DrivAerNet++ parametric table. None is present here, so no part of this design has been executed and none can be executed as-is in this workspace. Also required: a fixed seed manifest and a versioned config manifest, so \"pre-registered\" is verifiable.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Analysis plan and pre-registered decision rules\n\nObservation unit: per-(arch, N, seed) test metric → 280 cells × 5 seeds = 1,400 primary observations. CIs: paired test-set bootstrap (10,000 resamples, identical resampled indices across architectures and N, preserving paired comparisons) as the primary interval; seed-level bootstrap as the secondary interval.\n\n**(a) \"Performance improves with training-set size\" — per architecture.** Declared *supported* iff all three hold, with Holm–Bonferroni across the 7 trainable arms:\n1. Spearman ρ(test RMSE, log₁₀N) averaged over seeds ≤ **−0.90**, with bootstrap 95% CI upper bound < 0;\n2. Relative RMSE reduction from `N_min=50` to `N_max` ≥ **5 %** *or* absolute ≥ **1 Cd count**, whichever is larger, with paired bootstrap CI excluding 0;\n3. At `N_max`, RMSE beats A0 (train-mean) by the paired seed-level bootstrap with p < 0.05 (i.e. the arm has signal at all).\n\n**Saturation label (pre-registered, so a flat curve is not silently called \"no improvement\"):** if (1) fails but RMSE(N_max) is within **2 %** of RMSE(N=1,600), the arm is reported as *saturated / scaling exponent unidentifiable over the observed ladder*, not as *size-insensitive*.\n\n**(b) \"Scaling differs across architectures.\"** Declared *supported* iff all hold:\n1. Interaction significant: `RMSE ~ log10(N) * architecture + (1|seed)` (linear mixed model, log-RMSE as a robustness variant), Wald/LRT on the interaction p < 0.05 with Holm correction;\n2. At least one pair of architectures has power-law exponents `b` differing by a bootstrap CI excluding 0, where `L(N) = L∞ + a·N^(−b)` is fit per arm by nonlinear least squares on seed-averaged curves (report `b` ± CI; report `L∞` and flag it as weakly identified whenever `L∞ < 0` or its CI spans 0 — an expected and reported limitation of 8-point ladders);\n3. Practical significance: seed-averaged RMSE gap ≥ **1 Cd count** at ≥ 2 ladder points with paired CI excluding 0.\n\n**Rank stability and crossovers.** Kendall's W over architectures within each N, plus its bootstrap CI; the best-performing arm per N; **crossover N\\*** where A4 and A5/A6 curves cross, estimated by interpolation with a bootstrap CI, reported only if CI lies inside [50, N_max] — otherwise the statement is \"no crossover within the observed range\". Secondary descriptive: error-vs-N curves with bands, per-body-variant error decomposition, A2 uncertainty vs N.\n\n**Control expectations (fixed in advance).** A-null must be flat in N and ≈ A0-level; if A-null *improves* with N, the subset-generation or evaluation code is broken and no claim is made. A0 defines the no-signal floor every curve is plotted against. Per-subset scaler refit and log-Cd must not change any conclusion in (a)/(b); if they do, the conclusion is reported as preprocessing-dependent.\n\n**Secondary regime arm (does not touch the primary grid).** Two regimes with per-regime frozen TEST definitions: (R1) interpolation — the primary split; (R2) hold-a-body-type-out — one body variant removed entirely from TRAIN/VAL and used as TEST `[A-5]` (which variant held out is fixed in the manifest before running; all three held out one at a time is the preferred version if budget allows). Arms A4 and A5 only, sizes {100, 400, 1600, N_max(R2)} (the ladder is truncated because removing a body variant shrinks the pool), 3 seeds. Pre-registered expectation to test rather than assume: the A5-vs-A4 gap widens in R2 relative to R1 (parametric extrapolation across body styles penalizes deep nets more than trees). Claim from this arm is descriptive; it cannot rescue or weaken the primary claims.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Assumptions requiring confirmation\n\n`[A-1]` Pool ≈ 10,000 samples → 8k/1k/1k split; also fixes `N_max`. `[A-2]` Target column is total Cd; CdA only as fixed-area rescaling. `[A-3]` The 26 parameters exclude driving conditions; sufficient nominal-yaw samples exist. `[A-4]` Cd range ≈ 0.23–0.50 (drives the choice of raw scale over a transform). `[A-5]` Body variant is an explicit parameter enabling stratification and the hold-out regime. `[A-6]` No released canonical parametric split must be honored. `[A-7]` CFD-convergence/noise floor is ≤ ~2 Cd counts. All of these come from external recall, not from this repository, which contains none of them.\n\n## 7. Limitations\n\n1. **Unverifiable data specifics here** — sample count, parameter names/bounds, target definition, body-variant encoding, yaw bookkeeping. If `[A-1]` is much smaller (e.g. a 5k pool), the top of the ladder compresses and exponent estimates weaken; if `N_max < 2,000`, power-law fitting should be replaced by monotone-trend + paired-difference claims only.\n2. **Early saturation / noise floor.** If CFD repeatability is ~1–3 counts and the models reach it by N≈1,600, the informative region of the ladder is short, `L∞` and `b` become collinear, and architecture ranking can be decided inside the noise. Mitigations: the explicit noise-floor probe, the pre-registered saturation label, and paired bootstrap CIs rather than point-curve eyeballing.\n3. **Exponent estimation from 8 log-spaced points** is fragile: `b` is reported with CIs and alongside the model-free Spearman/p